what's goodie welcome back to hot breath  the show where you learn comedy from the  pros  that is your host guinness i am your  co-host  comedian joel byers and this channel is  all about helping you learn comedy from  the pros and become part of the next  generation of great comics i've  interviewed over 300 comedians on this  channel  and get a lot of questions from you and  a big one i keep getting is about  creating a tight five-minute set and  today's topic  is act outs it is adding  that extra dimension to your performance  so i have a few quick tips for you to  help you level up your type 5  and also a bonus one at the end all  about impression so the first  one is to show don't  tell a lot of the times we use a lot of  words  to describe something that we could  easily just perform or animate  instead and you don't have to be the  best actor i should say this all up for  you you don't have to be a trained actor  you don't have to be an actor  there are little cues you can do  that even if you aren't an actor you  don't do wild and crazy characters  there's little performance cues you can  do  to still add a new dimension and add  that acting  to your set so in showing and not  telling a big part is a lot of times we  may describe someone as  oh it was a a jovial a  rotund man who always came  in to work late um with his coffee  and he was panting because he ran inside  or whatnot  but what you could do instead is  actually if you're talking about  this rotund man you could  actually like physically show yourself  and that's  bill from work he always comes in late  like huff  huffing and puffing drinking his coffee  that you can actually instead of telling  us  what he's doing and how he looks while  doing it you can just show us  which takes me to the next point here  which is to actually  instead of narrating start acting  so a lot of the times we say this one  time at that one place so and so said  such and such to me  but a very effective way to actually  just take us to that moment the audience  is going to be way more connected when  it's in the present when you take us to  the scene  that you're describing to where you talk  to your co-worker  instead of saying oh last year at work i  saw bill come in late and i approached  him like hey bill you're late again and  he said well you know how i  am better late than never am i right  instead you could just take us to that  moment  and be like talking about work it'd have  to be a year ago you could  just say it just happened today or  whatever  and be say oh bill late again huh  well you know how i am that better late  than never  so both terrible act outs right there  but you can see  even as someone as terrible of an actor  as i am  just simple cues of saying a voice in  this direction looking in that direction  now signifies i'm speaking  saying the other voice in this direction  now notifies that  bill is speaking so just a simple  shift in your point of view  will indicate which character is talking  even if you can't throw your  voice and do actual characters so that's  a quick  way to even if you're not doing voices  looking this way one person speaking  while looking this way the other person  is speaking  is another way to do act out but the  biggest thing of all  in everything in my 11 years of comedy  what i have learned in doing act outs  the best tip is to start  incremental i always set a goal when i  do a set  of just a single goal of i want to try  this new punchline or i want to try this  new act out or on this joke  i'm just going to get absurd with the  character and see how much fun i can  have with it  and just start to build your act out  incrementally  go through your sets and in a previous  video i actually talked about  transcribing your sets to see where the  punch lines are  to where you can start editing them and  down but also go through your set and  see the opportunities to do  characters to bring in different voices  really start to look  through your set and how you can start  to add that extra dimension because it's  one thing  to really just be speaking and doing  your jokes  from this but when you actually start  bringing in your entire physicality  and start having that stage presence  that's when the jokes come to life and  that's when you really engage with the  audience  personal material is the perfect place  to start  and then when you bring in that personal  performance aspect to it  you're golden my friends so i hope that  really helps you in just a few minutes  here to just kind of  get out of that comfort zone of just  standing there reciting your jokes and  really starting to bring them to life  incrementally piece by piece now a lot  of people's is this bonus coming up  ask about impressions i've interviewed  several people who are really great at  impressions i'm personally not grading  impressions  but what i have learned from  interviewing so many people  is their number one tip is that you s  pick something specific about this  person  and then just exaggerate it for comedic  effects so if they have a funny voice or  if they're  rodney dangerfieldness hell ya i get no  respect  i think guinness is about tired of  watching me do these terrible  impressions  but you see it was rodney dangerfield  you kind of throw his voice a little bit  and do this exaggerating thing  it's just a very quick way to do  impressions so think about that when  you're doing your characters as well  even if you're not doing impressions  trying to figure out how to act out  someone think about something specific a  specific trait they have whether it's a  physical or vocal trait  and then just exaggerate it for comedic  effect it's a very  simple way to just add that new  energy into your set act outs really  bring an energy and a performance aspect  to your sets  that when you do see comics out there  really performing on stage and really  putting on  a show they all started somewhere and  they all started with first  learning the fundamentals and then  applying them  over time comedy is a marathon my  friends so just take these tips you hear  in this video  and our previous videos and all of our  interviews  and just apply things incrementally over  time  and in a few months of just incremental  shifts you're going to see a big  difference so i hope you found that  helpful  if you want to connect with me and  comics around the world in the hot  breath verse  go join our free facebook group where we  do all sorts of events and q and a's  and daily writing clubs all to help you  get better at comedy faster  we are all about being positive and  supportive and collaborative but  productive  you got to put in the work all of these  videos  that you watch on my channel or others  or you take courses or you read books  it only works if you do  now it's time to put in the work  go forth hop brother and sister and  apply these tips  leave a comment with your favorite tip  below and maybe how you're going to  apply to your next show  and i hope to see you in our facebook  group  dab on them guinness  get to work
 

